I found some coins that are not from here.

20
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:58,040
Oh yeah, those are from my trip to London.

21
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:01,640
I have like 90 pence but in all it won't add up to one pound.

22
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:04,360
Are you sure the bank will change these coins for you?

23
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:04,860
Hopefully.

24
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:14,880
All right, we're back so let's take a look at some of this language on Language Takeaway.

25
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:15,530
Language Takeaway.

26
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:22,050
So first up, Marco, we have this funny phrase, break the piggy bank.

27
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:23,030
What is a piggy bank?

28
00:01:23,290 --> 00:01:24,570
That's right, a piggy bank.

29
00:01:24,970 --> 00:01:28,330
Now you may have seen this, it has the shape of a little pig.

30
00:01:28,670 --> 00:01:37,090
It's usually made out of plastic or ceramic and it has a little hole at the top where you can put in coins and just basically save your money there.

31
00:01:37,470 --> 00:01:41,870
All right, so this is a pig-shaped bank but it's a bank you keep in your room.

32
00:01:42,290 --> 00:01:43,610
Mostly children have these, right?

33
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:55,820
Yeah, so if you want to save your change or you want to save some of your coins that you have or teach your children about saving money, then usually they have a little piggy bank where they can store their money.

34
00:01:55,820 --> 00:01:59,470
So when you break the piggy bank, usually these were made of ceramic.

35
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,720
So to spend the money, you have to decide when it's very, very special or important.

36
00:02:04,730 --> 00:02:08,610
So you have to break the entire bank and that's when you take all the coins you've been saving.

37
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,600
Right, exactly, because they didn't really have a little hole where you could take it out from.

38
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:17,630
So when you wanted to take your money out, you literally had to break it.

39
00:02:17,890 --> 00:02:18,450
Break it, all right.

40
00:02:18,710 --> 00:02:19,970
So that's the piggy bank.

41
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:22,110
But what do we store inside a piggy bank?

42
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:24,310
We don't store bills, do we?

43
00:02:24,530 --> 00:02:25,930
That's right, no, we store coins.

44
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:33,100
So this is an interesting word, bill, you know, like the name Bill, Bill Clinton, for example.

45
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:35,050
Bills are the paper money that we use.

46
00:02:35,490 --> 00:02:39,390
All right, but not capitalized like a name because this is a normal, a common noun.

47
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:42,170
Bills represent all paper money.

48
00:02:42,750 --> 00:02:44,950
So I could get, I could ask for change.

49
00:02:45,170 --> 00:02:46,630
Could I have the change in bills, please?

50
00:02:46,910 --> 00:02:47,150
That's right.

51
00:02:47,370 --> 00:02:51,930
And if I'm at a store, they'll give me bills, maybe a $5 bill, $10 bill.

52
00:02:52,370 --> 00:02:54,010
This also counts for other currencies.

53
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:58,560
So if I'm in China, I might get my renminbi, my yuan in bills.

54
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:01,280
So it's just a general name for paper money.

55
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:01,510
That's right.

56
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:05,210
So, but what we store in the piggy bank are coins.

57
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:15,110
Now talking specifically about the types of coins or the different amounts that these represent, let's take a look at those more in detail.

58
00:03:15,740 --> 00:03:17,580
All right, so first up we've got quarters.

59
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:23,720
Now this should be pretty easy if you know your time because a quarter is one-fourth of something.

60
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:29,400
So if we're talking about money, it's a quarter of a dollar or 25 cents.

61
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:29,600
That's right.

62
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:35,540
So 25 cents or a quarter of a dollar or one-fourth of a dollar, we just call it a quarter.

63
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:41,200
So you know, most machines or pay phones, you need to insert quarters.

64
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:42,200
That's right.

65
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:49,200
So we recognize the quarter because it's the biggest coin and it has a big eagle on one side and on the other side it has George Washington's head.

66
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:50,840
George Washington, that's right.

67
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:56,640
Now the next in line or the next coin that we're going to take a look at are dimes.

68
00:03:57,230 --> 00:03:57,590
Dimes.

69
00:03:57,750 --> 00:04:03,550
Now these are also recognizable because they're the smallest coins, but that does not mean they're the smallest amount.

70
00:04:04,130 --> 00:04:06,130
Dimes are actually the second largest.

71
00:04:06,470 --> 00:04:07,450
They're 10 cents.

72
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:10,130
They're 10 cents or one-tenth of a dollar.

73
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:13,930
And so you can say they're 10 cents or you can say dime.

74
00:04:14,310 --> 00:04:18,290
So do you have, I don't know, if you have 10 dimes, that means you've got a dollar.

75
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:18,910
That's right.

76
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:21,160
10 dimes equal one dollar.

77
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:23,510
And the face on the dime is Thomas Jefferson.

78
00:04:24,010 --> 00:04:24,690
Alexander Hamilton.

79
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:25,960
I don't know.

80
00:04:26,150 --> 00:04:29,350
No, he was the first, well, he created basically the Department of the Treasury.

81
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:32,930
But Thomas Jefferson is on another coin and that's what we're going to look at next.

82
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:33,750
That's right.

83
00:04:34,870 --> 00:04:35,290
The nickel.

84
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:38,050
The nickel is like the metal, you know, nickel.

85
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:43,590
And yeah, so this one is a little bit bigger than the dime, but not as big as the quarter.

86
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:46,280
I think it's a little bit thicker maybe.

87
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:48,670
It is thicker, but it's not as much money.

88
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:52,440
A nickel is five cents.

89
00:04:52,440 --> 00:04:52,680
Five cents.

90
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:54,760
So one-twentieth of a dollar.

91
00:04:55,040 --> 00:04:55,220
That's right.

92
00:04:56,040 --> 00:04:57,040
One-twentieth of a dollar.

93
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:59,120
So this is what we call a nickel.

94
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:02,880
So you would call this coin, you would say, hey, do you have any nickels?

95
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:04,360
Dimes and nickels usually go together.

96
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:05,520
I don't know why.

97
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:06,520
Nickels and dimes.

98
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:07,520
Nickels and dimes.

99
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:09,660
Nickels and dimes means he doesn't have very much money.

100
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:11,080
That's right.

101
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:16,860
So, yeah, and then on this coin you have the face of Thomas Jefferson.

102
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:17,540
That's right.

103
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:19,400
Okay.

104
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:22,200
And finally we have the only coin that's not silver colored.

105
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:23,910
It's actually more of a copper color.

106
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:25,830
That would be the penny.

107
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:26,470
The penny.

108
00:05:26,790 --> 00:05:26,970
That's right.

109
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:32,240
One cent or one penny is the smallest of the coins.

110
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:34,810
And actually you need 100 of these to make one dollar.

111
00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:35,470
That's right.

112
00:05:36,340 --> 00:05:38,840
And it's very unique because it's a different color.

113
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:41,880
It's the smallest amount.

114
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:43,800
And the face on this coin, do you know this one?

115
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:45,280
Yes.

116
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:46,480
This one is Abraham Lincoln.

117
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:46,720
Correct.

118
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:47,220
All right.

119
00:05:47,580 --> 00:05:50,120
So we've got all these important people.

120
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:53,260
All of them were presidents actually except for Alexander Hamilton.

121
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:53,940
That's right.

122
00:05:54,740 --> 00:05:57,380
And actually I think Alexander Hamilton looks good.

123
00:05:57,960 --> 00:05:58,420
Better than the rest.

124
00:05:58,880 --> 00:05:59,880
I don't know why.

125
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:00,880
He was a pretty handsome man.

126
00:06:00,880 --> 00:06:02,520
Actually, no, he was said to be very funny looking I think.

127
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:12,930
So actually all of these coins, you can call them by these names and everyone will recognize them, especially in the US.

128
00:06:13,480 --> 00:06:21,170
Now I don't really think they apply to any other country as we saw later on, for example, money in England or the United Kingdom.

129
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:28,070
But usually in the US, if you say, hey, do you have any spare change or do you have any nickels and dimes?

130
00:06:28,530 --> 00:06:29,390
Or can I borrow a penny?

131
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:30,350
Can I borrow a penny?

132
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:36,800
Usually in the US, they have these little things called take a penny, leave a penny, right?

133
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:37,250
Or give a penny, take a penny.

134
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:41,050
You see these because pennies are not worth very much money.

135
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:51,610
And oftentimes people will leave a penny and if you have, it may something costs 26 cents, but you only have a quarter, you can take a penny and then spend that.

136
00:06:52,280 --> 00:06:55,290
So they're basically free pennies you might see in a store.

137
00:06:55,520 --> 00:07:00,890
And then also if you get change, maybe you don't want to carry those pennies around or that change.

138
00:07:01,290 --> 00:07:03,110
You just leave that there.

139
00:07:03,480 --> 00:07:07,760
And then it's kind of a way of recycling this small amount of money, which is very interesting.

140
00:07:07,760 --> 00:07:09,600
It is very interesting.

141
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:12,070
But we're going to actually take a look at some British currency.

142
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:13,890
So let's take another listen to the dialogue.

143
00:07:13,890 --> 00:07:15,730
And when we come back, we'll explore that.

144
00:07:21,930 --> 00:07:23,070
Help me organize these coins.

145
00:07:23,730 --> 00:07:24,550
That's a lot of money.

146
00:07:25,130 --> 00:07:25,630
What'd you do?

147
00:07:25,870 --> 00:07:26,650
Break the piggy bank?

148
00:07:27,110 --> 00:07:28,970
Yeah, I'm going to go to the bank and change it for bills.

149
00:07:29,190 --> 00:07:31,430
But first I have to separate them into little piles.

150
00:07:31,990 --> 00:07:32,390
Okay.

151
00:07:32,970 --> 00:07:36,150
I'll find all the quarters and dimes while you sort the nickels and pennies.

152
00:07:36,510 --> 00:07:36,770
Great.

153
00:07:37,050 --> 00:07:39,190
Then we can add everything up and take it to the bank.

154
00:07:39,690 --> 00:07:41,570
I found some coins that are not from here.

155
00:07:41,810 --> 00:07:43,370
Oh yeah, those are from my trip to London.

156
00:07:43,830 --> 00:07:46,950
I have like 90 pence, but it won't add up to one pound.

157
00:07:47,470 --> 00:07:49,710
Are you sure the bank will change these coins for you?

158
00:07:49,910 --> 00:07:50,190
Hopefully.

159
00:07:54,310 --> 00:07:55,070
All right, we're back.

160
00:07:55,280 --> 00:08:00,640
So let's take a look at these British words on Language Takeaway Part 2.

161
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:04,730
So here we've got British currency, the pound, right, is the name of the currency.

162
00:08:05,070 --> 00:08:06,330
So in America we have the dollar.

163
00:08:06,330 --> 00:08:07,590
In Britain we have the pound.

164
00:08:07,850 --> 00:08:09,170
Is it the sterling pound?

165
00:08:09,470 --> 00:08:10,110
Yeah, pound sterling.

166
00:08:10,360 --> 00:08:11,360
The pound sterling.

167
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:12,360
Pound sterling.

168
00:08:12,360 --> 00:08:15,080
And I think that has to do with weight, I'm sure.

169
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:18,360
But here we've got it divided into small and large quantities.

170
00:08:18,360 --> 00:08:21,760
So where we have coins, we might have pence.

171
00:08:21,760 --> 00:08:22,760
Sounds like cents.

172
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:23,150
Right, exactly.

173
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:32,240
And I think actually the penny might come from the British term pence.

174
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:32,830
I'm sure it does.

175
00:08:33,240 --> 00:08:37,210
They're very similar and the meaning is basically one hundredth of a pound.

176
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:38,110
Right, exactly.

177
00:08:38,680 --> 00:08:42,640
And a pound is basically what you would say in the US, a dollar.

178
00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:44,810
A pound is the currency of England.

179
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:52,240
But actually, you know how in the United States people would say, you know, a hundred bucks, which would be a hundred dollars.

180
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:53,610
Over there they say one hundred quid.

181
00:08:53,960 --> 00:08:54,960
Quid.

182
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:56,040
So yeah, this is actually important slang.

183
00:08:56,760 --> 00:08:59,480
In America I would spend five bucks at the movies.

184
00:09:00,440 --> 00:09:02,460
In Britain I would spend five quid.

185
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:05,120
And now that's a slang term for one pound.

186
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:05,300
That's right.

187
00:09:06,120 --> 00:09:09,680
And actually I've heard also like 50p, which I think means 50 cents.

188
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:10,980
Yeah, pence.

189
00:09:11,120 --> 00:09:12,120
Pence.

190
00:09:12,120 --> 00:09:13,120
Yeah, so 50p.

191
00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:17,040
So you could say this stick of, or this pack of gum costs 50p.

192
00:09:17,140 --> 00:09:17,880
So 50 pence.

193
00:09:18,280 --> 00:09:18,620
That's right.

194
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:20,440
Half of a pound.

195
00:09:20,440 --> 00:09:26,770
So currency, obviously the currencies are different, but also it is interesting to learn the way that you would say it locally.

196
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:36,040
So you know, next time if you are in Britain or you're talking to an English person, then you'd know if they say, you know, 50 quid, you understand they're talking about 50 pounds.

197
00:09:36,040 --> 00:09:37,040
Exactly.

198
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:38,090
So, but they don't use the euro over there.

199
00:09:38,510 --> 00:09:39,270
No, they don't.

200
00:09:39,330 --> 00:09:40,230
Not in Great Britain.

201
00:09:40,430 --> 00:09:43,990
They do use the euro on the continents of France, Italy.

202
00:09:44,530 --> 00:09:46,930
And the euro is the name of that currency.

203
00:09:47,530 --> 00:09:47,810
Okay.

204
00:09:48,470 --> 00:09:48,710
All right.

205
00:09:49,520 --> 00:09:50,520
So that's good stuff.

206
00:09:50,520 --> 00:09:55,290
And now lastly, although we saw it at the beginning of the dialogue, let's talk about what this person wants to do.

207
00:09:55,360 --> 00:09:58,290
He wants to change these coins into bills.

208
00:09:59,030 --> 00:09:59,270
That's right.

209
00:09:59,410 --> 00:10:03,410
So the verb when you need to exchange money is to change money.

210
00:10:03,980 --> 00:10:08,440
So let's say if I go to England and all I have is dollars, I would say I want to change money.

211
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:10,000
Okay.

212
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:14,160
You could also say exchange, but when we're speaking to one another, we normally just say change.

213
00:10:14,560 --> 00:10:15,560
Right.

214
00:10:15,560 --> 00:10:16,560
Right.

215
00:10:16,560 --> 00:10:18,120
Because even the places are sometimes called like money changers.

216
00:10:18,120 --> 00:10:19,840
You know, they say that or something like that.

217
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:20,840
Exactly.

218
00:10:20,840 --> 00:10:26,680
And so if I want to change money, that means I want to maybe change dollars for euros or dollars for pounds.

219
00:10:26,680 --> 00:10:32,180
But this also can mean I want to change a dollar for some quarters.

220
00:10:33,040 --> 00:10:33,220
Right.

221
00:10:33,640 --> 00:10:34,640
Right.

222
00:10:34,640 --> 00:10:37,160
Because I need quarters for like a laundry machine or a telephone.

223
00:10:37,160 --> 00:10:38,160
Right.

224
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:40,840
And I've heard also the term is to break.

225
00:10:41,680 --> 00:10:50,490
So if maybe you have a $10 bill and you need maybe a $5 bill, but you don't have like, you ask the person to break that money.

226
00:10:50,490 --> 00:10:52,410
So you say, can you break this bill?

227
00:10:52,850 --> 00:10:53,130
Yeah.

228
00:10:53,170 --> 00:10:57,430
And that actually makes sense because a bill normally you break something that's big into smaller pieces.

229
00:10:57,870 --> 00:11:01,370
So if you think about it, if I break a glass, it becomes many pieces.

230
00:11:01,950 --> 00:11:05,050
Well, if I break a $5 bill, I want maybe $1.

231
00:11:05,690 --> 00:11:06,410
I get five of those.

232
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:08,790
There's one piece and then it breaks into many pieces.

233
00:11:09,660 --> 00:11:11,850
So to break means to ask for smaller change.

234
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:12,570
That's right.

235
00:11:12,850 --> 00:11:19,230
So maybe next time you guys need change, you can go up to someone and say, excuse me, can you change this $5 bill for me?

236
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:22,080
Or can you break this $5 bill for me?

237
00:11:22,080 --> 00:11:23,080
That's right.

238
00:11:23,080 --> 00:11:24,080
All right.

239
00:11:24,080 --> 00:11:25,520
So a lot of interesting language, very useful stuff.

240
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:27,030
Let's listen to the dialogue one last time.

241
00:11:33,550 --> 00:11:34,670
Help me organize these coins.

242
00:11:35,390 --> 00:11:36,150
That's a lot of money.

243
00:11:36,750 --> 00:11:37,250
What'd you do?

244
00:11:37,490 --> 00:11:38,250
Break the piggy bank?

